# This tree is published. Everything below either holds a secret, points at OUR deployment, or
# is build output, and none of it belongs in a public repo.
#
# The publish script (publish-selfhost.sh) works from an allowlist and does not rely on this
# file, deliberately: two independent mechanisms, because the cost of one of them being wrong
# once is a live database id on the internet.

# The REAL deployment configs. These carry the live D1 database ids of our hosted deployment.
# Untracked on purpose, and kept on disk so `wrangler deploy` still works from a dev machine.
# A fresh clone has to recreate them from wrangler.example.jsonc, which is tracked.
messaging-worker/wrangler.jsonc
files-worker/wrangler.jsonc

# Throwaway configs for the custom-servers end-to-end run.
messaging-worker/wrangler.test.jsonc
files-worker/wrangler.test.jsonc

# Local secrets: APNs signing keys, admin tokens, Turnstile keys.
.dev.vars
**/.dev.vars

# A filled-in on-prem config holds ADMIN_TOKEN and the enrollment hashes.
# config.capnp.example IS tracked; this is the copy an operator edits.
# Under Docker it is derived at container start and never written to the host at all.
config.capnp

# The Docker deployment's two. .env.example IS tracked; .env is the copy an operator edits, and
# secrets.env holds the generated admin token and durable-object keys.
.env
secrets.env

# Throwaway workerd configs written by the test scripts.
.test-*.capnp

# Build output: bundles and the generated capnp module lists.
dist/

# Dependencies, and wrangler's local state.
node_modules/
.wrangler/
.wrangler-dev-state/

# Durable-object state and blobs, if anyone runs a box from inside the checkout.
do-messaging/
do-files/
blobs/

.DS_Store
*.log
